        When recollecting in this manner, the yogi apprehends the continuity of nāma and rūpa in previous lives, tracing it sequentially from the most recent past existence back in due order. In this context, the term “anussarati” means “one recollects.” Through the exercise of his knowledge, the yogi recollects previous existences either in terms of the aggregates (khandhapaṭipāṭiyā) or in accordance with the process of death and rebirth (cutipaṭisandhi vasena), repeatedly and in due sequence. At this juncture, the yogis who recollect the aggregates of former births must be known in detail. 
    
    -   The Yogis Who Recollect Previous Existences
    
        There are six categories of individuals who are capable of recollecting the aggregates (khandha) 
        and associated phenomena (dhamma) of past existences according to the extent of their spiritual 
        attainment. These are: non-Buddhist ascetics (titthiyas), ordinary disciples (pakati sāvaka), Great 
        Disciples (Mahā Sāvaka), Chief Disciples (Agga Sāvaka), privately awakened Buddhas 
        (Paccekabuddha), and perfectly enlightened Buddhas (Sammā Sambuddha). The distinctions 
        concerning their respective knowledge of pubbenivāsānussati ñāṇa should be understood as 
        follows. 
    
    -   Time Distinction
    
        The temporal limits applicable to each category of individuals capable of recollecting previous 
        existences through pubbenivāsānussati ñāṇa are as follows. 
        
        (a). Titthiyā — Non-Buddhist ascetics and recluses, who adhere to doctrines such as 
        kammavāda and kriyāvāda believing in the phenomenon of kamma and its results, are able 
        to recollect past lives only up to a maximum of forty mahākappas. Beyond this limit, they 
        are unable to recall any further existences. The Brahmajāla Sutta records that certain non
        Buddhist ascetics recollected up to forty mahākappas. The limitation in their recollective 
        capacity is attributed to their deficiency (weakness) in wisdom. Specifically, they have not 
        attained nāmarūpapariccheda ñāṇa. Therefore, even the non-Buddhist ascetics who have 
        not attained the nāmarūpapariccheda ñāṇa are considered to have a weaker wisdom. 
        From this it should be understood that the cultivation of vipassanā bhāvanā constitutes a 
        particularly beneficial and decisive condition for the full development and sharpness of 
        pubbenivāsānussati ñāṇa.6 
        
        (b). Pakati sāvakā – Pakati sāvakas are the ordinary disciples of the Buddha. They are capable 
        of recollecting past existences extending up to one hundred mahākappas or one thousand 
        kappas. This capacity is attributed to the strength and development of their wisdom (paññā 
        bala).  
        Pakati sāvakas are the disciples of the Tathāgata who are neither Mahā Sāvakas (Great 
        Disciples) nor Agga sāvakas (Chief Disciples), but comprise the remaining disciples within 
        the Buddha’s dispensation. 
        
        (c). Mahā Sāvakā – The eighty great disciples are capable of recollecting past existences 
        extending up to one hundred thousand mahākappas.  
        
        (d). Agga Sāvakā – The two Chief Disciples are capable of recollecting past existences extending back to one asaṅkheyya and one hundred thousand mahākappas. vatvā taṃ 6Yasmā titthiyānaṃ brahmajālādīsu cattālīsāya eva saṃvaṭṭavivaṭṭānaṃ anussaraṇaṃ āgataṃ, tasmā "na tato para"nti kāraṇaṃ vadanto "dubbalapaññattā"tiādimāha, pubbenivāsānussatiñāṇassa visesakāraṇanti dasseti. (Vsm-ṭ. II. 45.). tena vipassanābhiyogo 5 
        
        (e). Paccekabuddhā – Paccekabuddhas are capable of recollecting past existences extending back to two asaṅkhyeyyas and one hundred thousand mahākappas. The limitation regarding the recollection of past lives by the above-mentioned ordinary disciples, Great Disciples, Chief Disciples, and Paccekabuddhas depends upon the extent of their puññañāṇābhinīhāra. The term puññañāṇābhinīhāra denotes the period during which one cultivates wholesome merit (puñña) and knowledge (ñāṇa) directed towards the attainment of a Bodhi, following the initial aspiration (abhinīhāra).7 In other words, it refers to the duration of sustained meritorious and wisdom-based practice undertaken after formally resolving upon enlightenment. These Nobles are capable in recalling their period of fulfilling pāramīs. However, in every Buddha Sāsana there are four disciples who attain the state of mahābhiññappatta. These eminent disciples are capable of recollecting past existences extending back to one asaṅkheyya and one hundred thousand kappas. In the Sāsana of the Gotama Buddha, the four disciples who attained the status of mahābhiññāppatta were: 
        (i). Arahant Sāriputta Thera 
        (ii). Arahant Mahā Moggallāna Thera 
        (iii). Arahant Bakkula Thera 
        (iv). Arahant Bhadda Kāpilānī (Yasodharā) Therī 8 
        
        Among these four, the two Chief Disciples made their original aspiration for the position 
        of Chief Discipleship during the dispensation of Anomadassī Buddha, one asaṅkheyya and 
        one hundred thousand kappas ago. According to the ancient commentarial narratives 
        concerning Bakkula Mahā Thera, a significant meritorious deed was likewise performed 
        during the Sāsana of Anomadassī Buddha. It may therefore be reasonably inferred that the 
        formal fulfilment of that Thera’s pāramī began from that very period. 
        According to the Aṅguttara Aṭṭhakathā, Bhadda Kāccānā Therī (Yasodharā) was capable 
        of recollecting past existences extending up to one asaṅkheyya and one hundred thousand 
        kappas. However, according to the Apadānapāḷi, her power of recollection extended even 
        further into the past. The text records that she herself recalled and declared how she 
        received the fixed prediction (niyata vivaraṇa) to become the wife of the Bodhisattva who 
        will become the Gotama Buddha in her final existence (pacchima attabhāva). This 
        prediction was bestowed at the feet of Dīpaṅkara Buddha, right after the Bodhisattva 
        received his first fixed proclamation, four asaṅkheyyas and one hundred thousand kappas 
        ago, when she was born as a woman named Sumittā.9 
        Furthermore, in the Apadānapāḷi, she states that together with the Bodhisattva she 
        encountered previous Buddhas such as Brahmadeva Buddha, Gotama Buddha and many 
        others even prior to her first fixed prediction. Thus, the Apadānapāḷi tradition presents her 
        
        recollective knowledge as extending beyond the limits described in the commentarial 
        account. 
        Brahmadevañca sambuddhaṃ, gotamaṃ lokanāyakaṃ, 
        Aññesaṃ lokanāthānaṃ, saṅgamaṃ te bahuṃ mayā.10 
        Accordingly, the knowledge of recollection of previous existences (pubbenivāsānussati 
        ñāṇa) possessed by Bhadda Kāccānā Mahā Therī appears to have extended far beyond four 
        asaṅkheyyas and one hundred thousand kappas. 
        In addition, the group of eighteen thousand Arahant bhikkhunīs, together with Bhadda 
        Kāccānā Therī (Yasodharā), accompanied the Bodhisattva throughout their previous 
        existences while fulfilling the requisite perfections (pāramī). These bhikkhunīs are said to 
        have received the fixed prediction (niyata vivaraṇa) simultaneously with Yasodharā at the 
        feet of Dīpaṅkara Buddha, four asaṅkheyyas and one hundred thousand kappas ago. 
        According to the Apadānapāḷi, the entire group of eighteen thousand Arahant bhikkhunīs 
        was capable of recollecting that event precisely as it occurred. Moreover, the text records 
        that, even prior to the first fixed proclamation of the Bodhisattva, these bhikkhunīs together 
        with the Bodhisattva had encountered former Buddhas such as Brahmadeva Buddha, 
        Gotama Buddhas and many others in remote aeons of the past, just as Yasodharā. 
        On this basis, it may be inferred that those who had been consorts of the Bodhisattva during 
        his lay life and who later attained Arahantship as bhikkhunīs likewise possessed the 
        knowledge of recollection of previous existences (pubbenivāsānussati ñāṇa) extending 
        beyond four asaṅkheyyas and one hundred thousand kappas, just as Yasodharā Therī.11 
        Furthermore, it is recorded that, during the dispensation of Dīpaṅkara Buddha, four 
        asaṅkheyya and one hundred thousand kappas ago, a group of ten thousand ladies, together 
        with Bhadda Kāccānā (Yasodharā), made the aspiration to become consorts of the 
        Bodhisattva. They, too, became Arahants in their last lives during the Buddha Gotama’s 
        dispensation.12 It should be inquired that these ten thousand bhikkhunīs are to be included 
        or not within the larger group of eighteen thousand bhikkhunīs mentioned above, rather 
        than regarded as a separate assembly. 
        (f). Sammā Sambuddhā – With regard to Sammā Sambuddhas, no limitation is ascribed to 
        their recollection of previous existences. Just as the knowledge of omniscience 
        (sabbaññuta ñāṇa) is without restriction, so too the pubbenivāsānussati ñāṇa of a Sammā 
        Sambuddha. Whenever a Buddha directs his mind toward any past existence, he is able to 
        know it precisely as it occurred. 
        In other words, it should be considered that everyone including Sammā Sambuddhas have 
        a limit in their pubbenivāsanussati-ñāṇa. However, in the case of a Sammā Sambuddha, 
        this cannot be regarded as a genuine limitation. By virtue of the excessiveness of the 
        requisites of enlightenment (mahābodhi sambhāra), cultivated through skill in causes 
        (kāraṇa kosalla) and skill in means (upāya kosalla) there is no limit in the power of the 
        perfection of wisdom (paññā pāramī) of a Mahā Bodhisattva. The supernormal 
        knowledges (abhiññāṇa) arising through the power of such fully perfected wisdom are 
        entirely unrestricted (have no limitation).  
