        The mode in which past lives are contemplated (ārammaṇaggahaṇa) 
        The mode in which objects are taken by each yogi, who recollects previous existences through 
        pubbenivāsānussati ñāṇa (the knowledge of recollection of former abodes) should be understood as 
        follows. 
        This recollection occurs in three distinct modes. 
        (1). Khandhapaṭipāṭi – Recollection in the sequential order of the aggregates is termed khandhapaṭipāṭi. 
        This mode of recollection proceeds by first apprehending the moment of cuti (death-consciousness), 
        either of his own past existence or of another being, in the immediately preceding existence and 
        then tracing the aggregates retrospectively in reverse order, stage by stage.  
        Some teachers, however, have regarded the iriyāpatha paṭipāṭi as equivalent to khandhapaṭipāṭi. It 
        means aggregates arising in one physical posture must be considered as a single khandha. Recalling 
        nāma rūpa in one posture and then recalling the nāma rūpa in the immediately previous posture is 
        considered moving from one khanda to another khandha.  
        (2). Cutipaṭisandhi vasena – When a yogi recollects the cuti (death-consciousness) either of his own 
        past existence or of another being, and without attending to the intervening dhammas in between 
        the cuti and paṭisandhi (rebirth-linking consciousness) of that live, directly apprehends the 
        paṭisandhi of that life, this mode of recollection is termed cutipaṭisandhi vasena. 
        In this manner, the yogi is able, through the power of knowledge, to move swiftly from one 
        existence to another. When there is no specific benefit in examining the intermediate life
        continuum in detail, recollection may be undertaken according to the method of cutipaṭisandhi.14 
        (3). Sīhokkanti – This mode of recollection, literally, “the lion’s leap”,15 refers to a distinctive mode of 
        recollection in which a yogi, through the power of knowledge, directly recalls a particular past 
        existence without proceeding sequentially through intervening lives. In this case there is no need 
        to recall the sequence of all past lives one by one. According to the Theravāda tradition, this mode 
        of recollection is exclusively attributable to a Sammā Sambuddha. 
        Among the individuals mentioned above, the titthiyas are capable of recalling past existences only in terms 
        of a sequential review of the aggregates (khandhapaṭipāṭi). They are unable to recollect in the mode of cuti
        paṭisandhi while bypassing the intermediate succession of aggregates. This limitation is due to their lack 
        of insight knowledge (vipassana ñāṇa) and the relative weakness of their cognitive penetration (wisdom). 
