        Consequently, their recollection proceeds in a gradual and linear manner, without the ability to leap directly 
        to a particular moment in the past according to their intention. This limitation may be compared to a blind 
        person’s walking without a walking stick. Blind people are unable to move freely as they wish, so too the 
        titthiyas, when recollecting former lives, are bound to the sequential order of aggregates and cannot 
        transcend it. 
        Ordinary disciples (pakati sāvakas) and Great disciples (mahā sāvakas) recollect past existences by 
        reviewing the sequence of aggregates (khandha) and, through their knowledge, proceed from one existence 
        to another by apprehending the transition as cuti–paṭisandhi (death and rebirth-linking). 
        Chief disciples (agga-sāvakas) and Paccekabuddhas, however, do not rely on such a sequential 
        recollection of the aggregates. Instead, they are capable of directly perceiving the moment of death (cuti) 
        in a particular existence and immediately discerning the corresponding rebirth-linking (paṭisandhi). In this 
        manner, they recollect former lives through cuti–paṭisandhi recollection. 
        Thus, these five categories of individuals are capable of recollecting past existences either by reviewing the 
        sequential order of the aggregates or by recollecting them through the mode of cuti–paṭisandhi. 
        For Sammā Sambuddhas, there is no need to recollect the sequence of aggregates or to move through 
        knowledge to each previous existence by way of cuti–paṭisandhi. If they wish to know a particular event 
        whether it occurred hundreds of thousands of kappas in the past or will occur in the future – that event 
        manifests directly before them. Their knowledge of recollecting previous existences operates through the 
        method known as sīhokkanti (“the lion’s leap”). 
        According to the general opinion, during the season of Sārada (season following the rains), at the noon, a 
        man who is in a house measuring four cubits in size can see all the objects within the house. Even such a 
        person is unable to apprehend extremely subtle objects or those concealed behind a wall; such objects are 
        not within his visible range. In contrast, for the Sammā Sambuddhas there is nothing that is unperceivable 
        with their wisdom which he wishes to know. Whatever they wish to know becomes immediately accessible 
        to their knowledge. When a Buddha directs his mind to know something that object becomes exceedingly 
        clear and radiant in his knowledge, appearing with perfect clarity like an amla fruit placed on the palm of 
        the hand. The reason for this is that the Buddhas have completely eradicated the defilement together with 
        habitual tendencies known as ñeyyāvaraṇa. 
        Therefore, when Sammā Sambuddhas wish to know a particular existence, they are able to traverse 
        countless crores of aeons with their knowledge and directly apprehend that existence. In doing so, they may 
        bypass innumerable intervening aeons and focus directly on the specific object they intend to know. This 
        may be illustrated by the manner in which a monk recites a Sutta using peyyāla (an abbreviated form that 
        denotes elided repetition). Instead of reciting the entire sequence in order, he may simply recite the 
        beginning and the end, saying: “paṭhamaṃ jhānaṃ … pañcamaṃ jhānaṃ.” In a similar way, Sammā 
        Sambuddhas, by means of their knowledge, can pass over hundreds, thousands, or even millions of aeons 
        and directly recollect a particular event or existence in the past.16 
