        Likewise, Sammā Sambuddhas recollect previous existences directly by means of their knowledge, 
        employing the method known as “sīhokkanti.” 
        While thus proceeding, the knowledge of a Sammāsambuddha does not undergo “sajjana” nor “virajjana” 
        between successive existences. Rather, it proceeds directly to the particular point intended whether an aeon, 
        a specific existence, or a particular event. This may be compared to an arrow released by a highly trained 
        and skilled archer – such as the Bodhisatta Sarabhaṅga who strikes precisely his intended target without 
        becoming entangled in intervening trees, creepers, or other obstacles. In the same manner, the 
        Sammāsambuddhas are able, whenever they wish, to recall the aggregates pertaining to any particular 
        existence instantly and without obstruction. 
        When a certain existence is intended to be recalled, being unable to reach that existence with wisdom is 
        called “sajjana.” When a certain existence is intended to be recalled, being going beyond that existence and 
        recalling an existence which is further past is called “virajjhana.”17 
        Distinctive power (ānubhāvavisesa) 
        There is a distinctive power (ānubhāva visesa) associated with pubbenivāsānussati ñāṇa. The way past life 
        object manifest to the mind is decided according to the degree of this power. For titthiyas, the vision in the 
        recollection of past lives appears like the faint light of a firefly, since they lack the support of vipassana 
        ñāṇa. The vision of ordinary disciples (pakati sāvakas) resembles the light of a lamp. The vision of Great 
        disciples (Mahā Sāvakas) is comparable to the brightness of a blazing firebrand. The vision of the Chief 
        disciples (Agga Sāvakas) is like the radiance of the star Osadhī (the planet Venus, the morning star). The 
        vision of a Paccekabuddha resembles the brilliance of the moon. However, the vision of a 
        Sammāsambuddha is supremely clear, comparable to the radiance of the autumn sun (sarada sūriya), 
        which shines with a thousand rays, completely dispelling darkness in every direction. In this manner, the 
        knowledge of each attains its distinctive clarity according to its own degree of excellence. 
        Mode of Existence (pavatti ākāra) 
        This expression refers to the manner in which knowledge proceeds from one existence to another in the 
        process of recollection of past lives. 
        The recollection of past lives by titthiyas is comparable to the journey of a blind person who proceeds by 
        holding the end of a stick. This is because they are unable to recall any existence without following the 
        sequential order of the aggregates (khandha paṭipāṭi). The recollection of ordinary disciples (pakati 
        sāvakas) is like travelling across a narrow footbridge. The recollection of Great disciples (Mahā Sāvakas) 
        resembles a journey over a broader bridge constructed with firmly fixed planks, across which four or five 
        persons may pass at once. The recollection of the Chief disciples (Agga Sāvakas) is comparable to 
        travelling along a cartway. The recollection of a Paccekabuddha is like travelling on a wide foot road 
        along which even caravan of carts may travel comfortably. However, the recollection of a 
        Sammāsambuddha is like travelling along a vast highway upon which twenty or thirty carts may move 
        simultaneously.  
        Since the above discussion concerns the pubbenivāsānussati abhiññā in relation to disciples, the term 
        “anussarati” here indicates that the yogi recollects past existences either by following the sequence of 
        aggregates (khandhapaṭipāṭi) or by proceeding through the modes of death and rebirth (cuti–paṭisandhi). 
