        Subsequently, in the statement “Seyyathidaṃ – ekampi jātiṃ dvepi jātiyo&#x2026;” the intended meaning is that 
        the aggregates belonging to previous existences are recollected in the manner explained above. 
        Here, the expression “one birth” (ekaṃ jātiṃ) refers to the aggregates that arise between the rebirth and the 
        death within a single existence. In the same way, the expressions “two births,” “three births,” and so forth 
        should be understood as referring to the aggregates belonging to two, three, and other successive existences 
        respectively. 
        Asaṅkheyya kappa 
        Thus, the yogi who recollects past lives contemplates both the saṃvaṭṭa kappa and the vivṭṭa kappa. The 
        saṃvaṭṭa kappa refers to a period in which the world declines and undergoes destruction. The vivaṭṭa kappa 
        refers to a period in which the world grows and gets created. By the term saṃvaṭṭa kappa, the saṃvaṭṭaṭṭhāyī 
        kappa (the time period that exists after the world’s destruction) is also taken, and by the term vivaṭṭa kappa, 
        the vivaṭṭaṭṭhāyī kappa (the time period that exists after the world got made) is also taken. The 
        saṃvaṭṭaṭṭhāyī kappa is based upon the saṃvaṭṭa kappa, whereas the vivaṭṭaṭṭhāyī kappa is based upon the 
        vivaṭṭa kappa. 
        Accordingly, the terms saṃvaṭṭa kappa and vivaṭṭa kappa are used to explain the four asaṅkheyya kappas 
        described in the Kappa Sutta of the Aṅguttara Nikāya.  
        Kappa Sutta 
        Cattārimāni, bhikkhave, kappassa asaṅkhyeyyāni. Katamāni cattāri? 
        Yadā, bhikkhave, kappo saṃvaṭṭati, taṃ na sukaraṃ saṅkhātuṃ – ettakāni vassānīti vā, ettakāni 
        vassasatānīti vā, ettakāni vassasahassānīti vā, ettakāni vassasatasahassānīti vā. 
        Yadā, bhikkhave, kappo saṃvaṭṭo tiṭṭhati, taṃ na sukaraṃ saṅkhātuṃ – ettakāni vassānīti vā, ettakāni 
        vassasatānīti vā, ettakāni vassasahassānīti vā, ettakāni vassasatasahassānīti vā. 
        Yadā, bhikkhave, kappo vivaṭṭati, taṃ na sukaraṃ saṅkhātuṃ – ettakāni vassānīti vā, ettakāni vassasatānīti 
        vā, ettakāni vassasahassānīti vā, ettakāni vassasatasahassānīti vā. 
        Yadā, bhikkhave, kappo vivaṭṭo tiṭṭhati, taṃ na sukaraṃ saṅkhātuṃ – ettakāni vassānīti vā, ettakāni 
        vassasatānīti vā, ettakāni vassasahassānīti vā, ettakāni vassasatasahassānīti vā. 
        Imāni kho, bhikkhave, cattāri kappassa asaṅkhyeyyāni. 
        For a Mahākalpa (Great Aeon), there are four asaṅkheyya kappas, which are classified as follows: 
        (1). Saṃvaṭṭa kappa – the period in which destruction occurs. During this period, the world system is 
        destroyed. 
        (2). Saṃvaṭṭaṭṭhāyī kappa – the period following the destruction, in which the world remains in the 
        state after the destruction. Actually, the physical world system does not exist during this period.  
        (3). Vivaṭṭa kappa – the period in which arising and growth of the world system occurs. During this 
        period, the world system is newly formed and gradually develops. 
        (4). Vivaṭṭaṭṭhāyī kappa – the period in which, after arising and growing, the world remains in that 
        formed state. 
        Among these, there are three modes of destruction (saṃvaṭṭa). 
        (1). Āpo saṃvaṭṭo – destruction by water 
        (2). Tejo saṃvaṭṭo – destruction by fire 
        (3). Vāyo saṃvaṭṭo – destruction by wind 
        11 
        Moreover, there are three “saṃvaṭṭa sīmā” that is, the limits up to which cosmic destruction extends. 
        (1). Ābhassara sīmā – This refers to the boundary, which ends with the Ābhassara Brahma realm. 
        When a kappa is destroyed by fire [destruction of a kappa means the destruction of a world system], 
        all realms below the Ābhassara plane are destroyed. Accordingly, the entire kāma loka (realm of 
        sensual pleasure) and the three Brahma realms of the first jhāna are destroyed, whereas none of the 
        second-jhāna Brahma realms are destroyed. 
        (2). Subhakiṇha sīmā – This refers to the boundary, which ends with the Subhakiṇha Brahma realm. 
        When a kappa is destroyed by water, all realms below the Subhakiṇha plane are destroyed. 
        Accordingly, the entire kāma loka (realm of sensual pleasure), the three Brahma realms of the first 
        jhāna and the three Brahma realms of the second jhāna are destroyed, whereas none of thrid-jhāna 
        Brahma realms are destroyed.18 
        (3). Vehapphala sīmā – This refers to the boundary, which ends with the Vehapphala Brahma realm. 
        When a kappa is destroyed by air, all realms below the Vehapphala plane are destroyed. 
        Accordingly, the entire kāma loka (realm of sensual pleasure), the three Brahma realms of the first 
        jhāna, the three Brahma realms of the second jhāna and the three Brahma realms of the third jhāna 
        are destroyed, whereas none of fourth-jhāna Brahma realms are destroyed.19 
        The saṃvaṭṭa sīmā is described as a “mariyādā” meaning a boundary or limit. That is, when cosmic 
        destruction (saṃvaṭṭa) occurs, the destruction extends only up to the specified plane, while the plane that 
        marks the boundary itself is not destroyed. For this reason, it is also referred to as “saṃvaṭṭana mariyādā,” 
        the boundary that demarcates the extent of cosmic destruction.20 
        In all such saṃvaṭṭa (cosmic destructions), the one trillion world-systems that constitute the Buddha’s 
        sphere of authority (āṇā khetta) also perish. When a new kappa arises (arising of a kappa means the arising 
        and formation of a world system) again all those destroyed world systems arise together. 
        What is the reason for the above-mentioned cosmic destruction? The occurrence of such cosmic destruction 
        ultimately has its basis in the unwholesome roots (akusala mūla). 
        The natural cessation of saṅkhāra (saṅkhārānaṃ sarasanirodho) occurs naturally. No cause is needed for 
        such cessation or destruction. However, the cessation of the continuity of aggregates beings pertaining to 
        various groups (satta nikāya) does not occur without causes and conditions. Therefore, the destruction of 
        the material world (bhājana loka) must also occur dependent upon specific causal conditions. 
        Here, the bhājana loka refers to the physical world that serves as the supporting basis for beings who dwell 
        and move within it; it is the spatial world (okāsa loka).21 
