        The arising of such a world-system occurs due to the meritorious power (puñña bala) of the beings who are 
        to be born there. Subsequently, through the unwholesome or evil power (pāpa bala) of those same beings, 
        who were born in a world-system, that world-system eventually undergoes destruction.22 
        When the unwholesome roots (akusala mūla) arise abundantly in beings, the world-system undergoes 
        destruction through the agency of the great elements namely fire (tejo), water (āpo), and wind (vāyo). When 
        lust (rāga) becomes predominant, the destruction of the world occurs through fire. When hatred becomes 
        excessive, the destruction takes place by means of highly caustic alkaline water (khāra udaka), which is 
        exceedingly sharp and corrosive.23 
        Some teachers have said that when hatred becomes predominant the world is destroyed by fire, and when 
        lust becomes predominant it is destroyed by water. Their reasoning is based on an analogy concerning the 
        nature of these defilements. According to this interpretation, hatred is comparable to an evident enemy and 
        thus resembles fire in its destructive intensity; therefore, when hatred arises strongly, the world is said to 
        be destroyed by fire. In contrast, lust is likened to a concealed or less obvious enemy, and is compared to 
        caustic alkaline water (khāra udaka). Accordingly, when lust becomes predominant, the destruction of the 
        world is said to occur through such alkaline water.24 
        When the delusion becomes predominant the world is destroyed by air.  
        When the world-system undergoes destruction in this manner, it is destroyed by fire on seven consecutive 
        occasions. On the eighth occasion, the destruction occurs through water. Thereafter, the same sequence 
        repeats: the world-system is destroyed seven times by fire, followed by destruction by water on the eighth 
        occasion. With the repetition of this pattern, every eighth destruction takes place through water. 
        Accordingly, after seven such cycles of water-destruction, the world-system would have undergone 
        destruction fifty-six times in total. Subsequently, the world-system is again destroyed seven times by fire, 
        bringing the total number of destructions to sixty-three. Thereafter, although the occasion for destruction 
        by water arises, the waters are restrained. Instead, the air element (vāta dhātu), having obtained the 
        opportunity, brings about the destruction of the world-system, extending even to the Subhakiṇha Brahma 
        realm, whose lifespan has reached sixty-four great aeons (mahākappa) 
        According to the Sāratthadīpanī, lust is generally more abundant among beings. Therefore, the destruction 
        of the world by fire is considered to occur more frequently.25 
