        A monk who recollects the sequence of previous aggregates and past aeons  is still able, while recollecting 
        other past existences, to recall the various types of cosmic cycles, namely saṃvaṭṭa kappa (aeons of 
        destruction), vivaṭṭa-kappa (aeons of origin), and saṃvaṭṭa vivaṭṭa kappa (both aeons of destruction and 
        origin) occurring within each world-cycle. How is this to be understood? 
        He recollects in the following manner: “I have been in that particular saṃvaṭṭa kappa, in that specific 
        existence (bhava) or mode of generation (yoni) or destination (gati) or in the state of consciousness 
        (viññāṇatthiti) or in that realm of sentient beings (sattāvāsa) or in that particular assembly of beings 
        (sattanikāya).” This mode of recollection is referred to by the term “amutrāsiṃ.” 
        Here arises a key question: according to the passage in the Visuddhimagga, namely, “Tattha amutrāsinti 
        amumhi saṃvaṭṭakappe ahaṃ amumhi bhave vā yoniyā vā gatiyā vā viññāṇaṭṭhitiyā vā sattāvāse vā sattanikāye vā 
        āsiṃ” When destruction occurs within a saṃvaṭṭa kappa, how are beings born in the divine, human and 
        awful (duggati) realms affected? 
        The Visuddhimagga presents the matter in this way because, within a saṃvaṭṭa kappa (the aeon of 
        destruction), beings undergo transitions through death and rebirth in various realms of existence for certain 
        periods of time.27 For example, when the destruction of the world-system occurs by fire, the saṃvaṭṭa kappa 
        is considered to begin from the point at which the great destructive cloud (vipattikara mahāmegha) first 
        arises. Thereafter, beings may continue to exist for some time in the four hell realms, the human realm, and 
        six divine realms before the complete destruction takes place. 
        Otherwise, it is stated in this manner to indicate that, while a saṃvaṭṭa kappa (the aeon of destruction) is in 
        progress, the rebirth of beings continues to occur in various realms of existence.28 
        Alternatively, the indeclinable “vā” in the phrase “Tattha amutrāsinti amumhi saṃvaṭṭakappe ahaṃ 
        amumhi bhave vā yoniyā vā gatiyā vā viññāṇaṭṭhitiyā vā sattāvāse vā sattanikāye vā āsiṃ” conveys the 
        sense of “lutta niddiṭṭha.” In that, the other three asaṅkheyya kappas are also included indirectly.29 
        Accordingly, although the expression explicitly states “in such-and-such saṃvaṭṭa kappa,” it should be 
        understood as indicating that, through the knowledge of pubbenivāsānussati ñāṇa, the yogi is able to discern 
        how beings existed in various states of existence throughout the four asaṅkhyeyya kappas. 
        It is important to know the meaning of the words such as bhava, yoni and so on, which were mentioned 
        above. 
        (1). Bhava (existence) – The three forms of existence: kāma bhava, rūpa bhava and arūpa bhava. 
        (2). Yoni (modes of birth) – The four modes of birth: aṇḍaja, jalābuja, saṃsedaja and opapātika.  
        (3). Gati (destinies) – The five destinies: niraya, peat, tiracchāna, manussa and deva.  
        (4). Viññāṇaṭṭhiti (stations of consciousness) –The seven stations of consciousness such as 
        ekattakāyā ekattasaññī etc.  
