        Thereafter, the yogi recollects: “In that particular existence I was named Tissa or I was named Pussa.” This 
        idea is expressed by the term “evaṃnāmo.” 
        Similarly, when the yogi recollects, “In that existence I belonged to the Kaccāna clan or I belonged to the 
        Kassapa clan,” this idea is conveyed by the term “evaṃgotto.” 
        Thus, the yogi becomes capable of recollecting his own name, lineage, and other such particulars associated 
        with each respective existence. 
        Thus, if the yogi wishes to recollect the bodily colour and appearance, experiences of pleasure and pain, as 
        well as the long or short duration of life of beings in those respective realms, he is also able to recollect 
        them. 
        When the yogi recollects, “In that existence I was fair in color or I had a brown complexion,” this idea is 
        expressed by the term “evaṃvaṇṇo.”  
        Similarly, the yogi can recollect, “In that existence I was a layperson consuming rice and meat 
        (sālimaṃsodanāhāro), or I was an ascetic eating fruits fallen from trees (pavattaphalabhojano).”32 This 
        idea is conveyed by the term “evamāhāro.” 
        The yogi can recollect, “In that existence I was a being who experienced worldly comforts, or I was a being 
        who experienced spiritual pleasures.” This idea is expressed by the term “evaṃsukhadukkhapaṭisaṃvedī.” 
        Similarly, the yogi can recollect, “In that existence I had a lifespan of up to one hundred years, or I had a 
        longer lifespan of up to eighty-four thousand kappas.” Recollections of this kind are conveyed by the term 
        “evamāyupariyanto.” 
        Later, the yogi recollects, “After passing away from that existence, I was reborn in that specific existence 
        (bhava) or mode of generation (yoni) or destination (gati) or in the state of consciousness (viññāṇatthiti) or 
        in that realm of sentient beings (sattāvāsa) or in that particular assembly of beings (sattanikāya).” This idea 
        is conveyed by the term “So tato cuto amutra udapādiṃ.” 
        After, the yogi recollects, “In that specific existence (bhava) or mode of generation (yoni) or destination 
        (gati) or in the state of consciousness (viññāṇatthiti) or in that realm of sentient beings (sattāvāsa) or in 
        that particular assembly of beings (sattanikāya), I was with such and such name, linage etc.”  
        Reflection in descending order (avarohaṇa paccavekkhaṇa) 
        The term “amutrāsiṃ” may also be explained in another way. According to this interpretation, when 
        recollecting the sequence of former births, the yogi begins with the existence immediately preceding the 
        present life and proceeds to recall earlier existences successively. However, the phrases “So tato cuto 
        amutra udapādiṃ” and “So tato cuto idhūpapanno” describe the process in descending order, referring to 
        the contemplation of rebirth as a being passes away from one existence and is born in another. Such 
