        kāmāvacara consciousnesses. 
        The term “idhūpapanno” in this context denotes: “I was born in the present existence in a family such as 
        that of a khattiya (noble/warrior) or a brāhmaṇa.” The term mentioned previously “So tato cuto” signifies: 
        “Having passed away from the existence immediately prior to the present life.” The earlier phrase “So tato 
        cuto amutra udapādi” conveys the meaning: “Having passed away from the existence that was two lives 
        prior to the present one, I was reborn in the existence immediately preceding the present life” Subsequently, 
        the passages beginning with “Tatrāpāsiṃ” indicating the recollection of details pertaining to that existence 
        prior to the present life, such as one’s name, lineage and related characteristics.33 
        The phrase “Iti sākāraṃ sauddesaṃ anekavihitaṃ pubbenivāsaṃ anussarati” signifies: “According to 
        what said earlier, the yogi recollects the various types of former aggregates which are with distinctive 
        characteristics (ākāra) and specific designations (uddesa).”  
        In this context, the term “uddesa” refers to the name and lineage. By means of name and lineage, a being 
        is designated (pointed out) as “Tissa,” and “Kassapa.” The term “ākāra”, on the other hand, denotes 
        distinguishing features such as bodily characteristics and so on. Accordingly, a being may be described (or 
        known) in various ways, for example, as “one of dark complexion” or “one of fair complexion,” and so 
        forth.34 
        The method of attaining pubbenivāsānussati abhiññā (preparatory 
        practice/meditation procedure) 
        A yogi who wishes to develop the supernormal knowledge of the recollection of former abodes 
        (pubbenivāsānussati abhiññāṇa) should first retire to a quiet and secluded place after taking his meal. There 
        he should progressively attain the form absorptions (rūpajjhāna) entering them sequentially from the first 
        jhāna to the fourth jhāna. Having done so, he should then enter the fourth jhāna that serves as the basis for 
        supernormal knowledge. After emerging from that attainment, he should begin the recollection by 
        reviewing the most recent action he performed, namely the act of sitting. Subsequently, he should recollect 
        his past actions in reverse order, tracing them step by step. For example, he should recall such actions as 
        preparing a seat, entering the monastery, placing the bowl and robes in their proper places, taking his meal, 
        returning to the monastery from the village, paying homage to the Stūpa and the Bodhi tree, washing the 
