        bowl, accepting the bowl, and so forth. From that point he should continue reviewing earlier actions: 
        washing his face, the activities performed in the early morning, the midnight period, early night period, and 
        in this manner, he should recollect all the actions performed throughout the entire night and the daytime. 
        Up to this stage, past actions become evident even to the ordinary consciousness (pakati viññāṇa). To the 
        consciousness associated with preparatory concentration, these events become eedingly distinct. 
        It is also considered sufficient for the meditator to enter directly into the fourth jhāna, which functions as 
        the basis jhāna for the attainment of supernormal knowledge, without necessarily successively entering all 
        four rūpajjhānas mentioned above. Nevertheless, the instruction to attain all four jhānas is given in order 
        to gladden and invigorate the mind and body through the rapture (pīti) and happiness (sukha) associated 
        with the lower three jhānas. 
        In the explanation, the recollection of “period/time” should be understood as referring to the review or 
        recollection of the actions performed during those respective periods of time. 
        When the yogi engages in recollection and finds that no particular action stands out or is clearly manifest, 
        he should re-attain the base jhāna and, upon emerging from it, attempt the recollection once more. In this 
        way, the obscure actions become manifest, as a dark room is illuminated by a newly lit lamp. Following 
        this method, the yogi should systematically recollect actions in reverse chronological order: those 
        performed on the second day, third day, fourth day, and fifth day, then those conducted within ten days, 
        half a month, a month, and subsequently all actions performed throughout the entire year. In continuation, 
        he should extend this recollection to actions over ten years, twenty years, and ultimately trace back to birth 
        in the present existence. Finally, the yogi should recollect the consciousness and matter (nāma rūpa) that 
        were present at the very moment of passing away (cuti khaṇa) in the former existences. 
        When practiced in this manner, a wise bhikkhu, who has cultivated merits with the wish of attaining 
        pubbenivāsānussati ñāṇa, is able, even upon the first recollection of the cuti of the former life, to mentally 
        remove the nāma rūpa at the moment of rebirth in this life (paṭisandhiṃ ugghāṭetvā) and apprehend the 
        mind and matter at the dying moment of the previous existence. 
        When a particular action does not become clearly manifest, the instruction to re-enter the base jhāna is 
        given because the sharpness of mindfulness and clarity of knowledge are intensified through the base jhāna. 
        If mindfulness and wisdom are likened to a weapon, the base jhāna functions as a whetstone that sharpens 
        it. 
        When recollecting the consciousness and matter at the moment of passing away in a previous existence, the 
        accepted view is that one should first recollect the matter and then the consciousness. However, some 
        teachers have stated that the consciousness should be recollected first, followed by the matter. 
        The consciousness and matter of the previous existence ceased entirely at the termination of that existence, 
        and new consciousness and matter arose in the present existence. Accordingly, the precise point of cessation 
        in the previous existence, referred to as the place where the former mind and matter were relinquished 
        (nikkhepapaṭṭhāna), is exceedingly difficult to apprehend and cannot be easily perceived, as it is a heavily 
        obstructed place, densely enclosed on all sides and extremely dark, making it hard to see. 
        Even during continuity of pavatti, mind and matter cease completely and new ones arise. However, 
        following the moment of passing away (cuti), the rebirth (paṭisandhi) occurs due to a new kamma (kamma 
        bhava), giving rise to new nāma rūpa in a new becoming/existence (upapatti bhava). The moment of 
        passing away belongs to one particular continuum (addhā paccuppanna), whereas the rebirth belongs to 
        another continuum (addhā paccuppanna).  
        Thus, for those of low wisdom, it is difficult to apprehend this moment. Therefore, even if the moment of 
        passing away (cuti) in a former existence is not clearly manifest on the first occasion, one should not 
        conclude, “I am incapable of applying recollection to discern the consciousness and matter at the moment 
        of cuti of the previous life.” Rather, one should repeatedly attain the base jhāna. By entering the base jhāna 
        17 
        and emerging from it, the yogi should repeatedly recollect the moment of passing away in the previous 
        existence, refining his insight through successive practice. 
        This preparatory practice (parikamma) is illustrated by the following simile: 
        A strong man attempts to cut down a large tree intended for the pinnacle of a building (kūṭāgāra kaṇṇikā). 
        When the branches and leaves are removed, the edge of his axe becomes blunt, and he is unable to sever 
        the main trunk. Undeterred, he proceeds to a smithy (kammārasālā) to have the axe sharpened and returns 
        to continue cutting. Whenever the axe becomes blunt again, he repeats the process, sharpening it and 
        resuming his work. In this manner, he cuts only those parts that have not yet been cut, leaving the already 
        severed part. By proceeding systematically in this way, he is able to put many trees down efficiently. 
        Similarly, a yogi, emerging from the base jhāna, does not repeatedly recollect the same moments already 
        recalled, but focuses only on the moment of passing away (cuti) in the previous existence. In this way, the 
        yogi soon becomes capable of recollecting the consciousness and matter (nāma rūpa) at the moment of 
        passing away having mentally removed the mind and matter that arose at the moment of paṭisandhi 
        (paṭisandhiṃ ugghāṭetvā). 
        In the foregoing simile, the peak of the building (kūṭāgāra) signifies the knowledge of the recollection of 
        former abodes (pubbenivāsānussati ñāṇa). The large tree symbolizes the mind and matter (nāma rūpa) of 
        the previous existence, while the branches and leaves attached to the tree correspond to the consciousness 
        (paṭisandhi citta) in the present existence connected nāma rūpas at the moment of passing away of the 
        former existence. The sharp edge of the axe represents the preparatory concentration (parikamma samādhi), 
        and the smithy denotes the base jhāna (pādaka jhāna). 
        This principle may also be illustrated by other similes, such as a splitting logs (kaṭṭhaphālaka) or a barber 
        engaged in cutting hair (kesohāraka). 
        When recollecting in this manner, the knowledge extending from the last act of sitting up to the moment of 
        rebirth (paṭisandhi) is not regarded as pubbenivāsānussatin ñāṇa. This is because the pubbenivāsānussati 
        ñāṇa cognizes the dhamma related to the previous life. The knowledge, which knows the phenomena of the 
        present life, is termed “parikamma samādhi ñāṇa.” This denotes the knowledge associated with the 
        preparatory concentration (parikamma samādhi). 
        Some teachers also maintained that parikamma samādhi ñāṇa may be referred to as “atītaṃsa ñāṇa.” 
        However, this designation should not be applied to the knowledge pertaining to the realm of fine forms 
        (rūpāvacara ñāṇa). The parikamma samādhi ñāṇa always belong to the realm of sensual pleasures 
        (kāmāvacara). Nowhere in the Canon or commentaries is it stated that a wisdom pertaining to the 
        rūpāvacara realm cognizing aggregates pertaining to the present life.  
        When recollecting in this manner, the yogi, having transcended the rebirth moment (paṭisandhi), directs his 
        attention to the mind and matter (nāma rūpa) that were present at the moment of passing away (cuti) in the 
        previous existence. At that time, the mind-door-adverting consciousness (manodvāravajjana citta) arises. 
        When it ceases, four or five successive consciousnesses arise, each directed toward the same mind and 
        matter of the death moment of the previous life. Among these, the first three or four are classified as 
        parikamma, upacāra, anuloma and gotrabhū. The fourth or fifth consciousness is an absorption mind 
        (appaṇā citta) associated with the fourth jhāna. The knowledge that arises along with this absorption 
        consciousness is termed the “pubbenivāsānussati ñāṇa” (knowledge of recollection of former abodes).  
